# The pre-commit floor. Fast, and honest about what is red by design.
#
# `set -e` is load-bearing. Husky runs this file with `sh`, and without it every
# command runs and only the LAST exit code decides: a Biome failure followed by a
# passing file-length scan commits anyway. A hook that reports success on a lint
# error is worse than no hook, because it is trusted.
set -e

# Lint, format and import order, on the staged files only. This is `check`, not
# `lint`: `check` is what `pnpm lint` runs, and a file that merely needs
# formatting fails it with no rule name in the output - the most confusing
# possible way to block a commit, so the hook runs the same thing the repo does.
#
# Warnings are allowed on purpose. The census, measured: 6
# `noExcessiveCognitiveComplexity` (core's cron ×3, diff, health, signals), 1
# fixable `useMathMinMax` (core's diff), and 2 `noImportantStyles` (the
# reduced-motion block in base.css, where `!important` is the whole point).
# Promoting them to errors blocks any commit that touches those files and teaches
# everyone to type `--no-verify`, which protects nothing.
#
# The guard is measured, not defensive. `biome check --staged` exits 1 when the
# staged set is EMPTY ("No files were processed in the specified paths"), which
# is what a message-only `git commit --amend` presents: the index equals HEAD, so
# there is nothing staged. Hit here, 2026-11-10: a commit whose message needed
# completing could not be amended at all, and the only way past the hook is
# `--no-verify` - the outcome the paragraph above exists to avoid. So the check
# runs when there is a file to check, and a commit with no content is not the
# thing a linter is for.
staged=$(git diff --cached --name-only --diff-filter=ACMR)
if [ -n "$staged" ]; then
  pnpm exec biome check --staged
fi

# Rule 1: no file over 300 lines. Whole tree, not staged files - a commit is a
# whole tree, and the rule is per-file, so a partial set can hide an overage.
node scripts/check-file-length.mjs

# Credentials, in every staged file (git ls-files reads the index, so staged work
# is covered). 0.13s, so it runs before the slow gates. This repo is public and
# needs no committed credential at all - the browser profile and the SQLite file
# are both gitignored - so a credential in a tracked file is a design violation,
# and a public commit is public forever (docs/decisions-tooling.md D-023).
node scripts/check-secrets.mjs

# The JSON Schema in schemas/ must match the zod schemas that generate it. Cheap,
# and drift here is silent everywhere else.
node scripts/generate-schemas.mjs check

# Typecheck, whole workspace: measured 1.9s (TypeScript 7 is the native compiler,
# D-018). A commit that does not compile is not a behaviour change, it is a
# broken tree, and 2 seconds is not a reason to let one exist.
pnpm typecheck

# The suite: measured 7.4s for `test:fast` (core, db, web, fetch). Rule 12 makes a
# test part of every behaviour, so the suite is the behaviour - committing a red
# one is how a red main becomes normal. The browser projects are NOT here: they
# launch a real Edge, 10.1s with them, and a hook that needs Edge installed cannot
# commit on a machine without it - and a hook that cannot pass gets bypassed.
pnpm test:fast

# Deliberately NOT `pnpm check`: that chain is minutes (per-package coverage, the
# Edge browser projects, `test:design`, the audit). `pnpm test:design` is green at
# HEAD — 12 computed assertions in real Edge, 2.4 s — and it stays out of the
# floor for the reason above: an Edge that is not installed exits 1, and an
# always-red hook is a hook that gets bypassed. `pnpm check` is the gate before
# declaring work done (AGENTS.md); this is the few-second floor that has to be
# green for a commit to exist at all.
